Subscribe and publish
const encode = (value: unknown) =&gt; new TextEncoder().encode(JSON.stringify(value))

actor.send({
  type: 'SUBJECT.SUBSCRIBE',
  config: { subject: 'demo.events', callback: (data) =&gt; console.log(data) },
})
actor.send({
  type: 'SUBJECT.PUBLISH',
  subject: 'demo.events',
  payload: encode({ message: 'hello' }),
  onPublishResult: (result) =&gt; {
    if (!result.ok) console.error(result.error)
  },
})
Subscriptions are keyed by subject; one target subscription is retained per subject. SUBJECT.UNSUBSCRIBE removes a subject, and SUBJECT.UNSUBSCRIBE_ALL clears the target set. Target configurations survive disconnect/reconnect so the subject manager can restore them on the new connection. Recreate the actor for a completely fresh demo session.
A successful publish callback means the client accepted the publish call. It is not a broker persistence or application-processing acknowledgement.

Request a reply
actor.send({
  type: 'SUBJECT.REQUEST',
  subject: 'demo.echo',
  payload: encode({ question: 'hello' }),
  opts: { timeout: 1500 },
  callback: (reply) =&gt; console.log('reply', reply),
  onRequestResult: (result) =&gt; {
    if (!result.ok) console.error('request failed', result.error)
  },
})
Real NATS needs a responder on the subject. The demo sandbox supplies a local responder only for demo.echo. A request failure is reported through onRequestResult; the reply callback runs on success. requestHeaders can asynchronously prepare additional headers before sending. When opts.timeout is provided, header preparation and the NATS request share that time budget.
The root obtains current credential headers for each request when configured with a credential adapter. User-provided requestHeaders are merged with those headers. Avoid logging authentication headers or placing credentials in the message body.

NatsConnectionConfig.auth supports userpass, token, and decentralised. Omit it for an unauthenticated development broker.
auth: { type: 'token', token: sessionToken }
// or:
auth: { type: 'userpass', user: username, pass: password }
// or sentinel credentials combined with user/password:
auth: { type: 'decentralised', sentinelB64, user: username, pass: password }
The live demo accepts an optional token, keeps it in memory, and does not save it to local storage or show it in actor details. Browser code cannot hide a credential from the person running that browser; use credentials scoped to the subjects and operations required.

